Abstract = {Ambient dose equivalent values were determined in several sites at the
   Instituto Nacional de Cancerologia, Department de Medicina Nuclear,
   using TLD-100 and TLD-900 thermoluminescent dosemeters. Additionally,
   ambient dose equivalent was measured at a corridor outside the
   hospitalization room for patients treated with Cs-137 brachytherapy.
   Dosemeter calibration was performed at the Instituto Nacional de
   Investigaciones Nucleares, Laboratorio de Metrologia, to known Cs-137
   gamma radiation air kerma. Radionuclides, considered for this study are
   I-131, F-18, Ga-67, Tc-99m, In-111, Tl-201 and Cs-137, with main gamma
   energies between 93 and 662 keV. Dosemeters were placed during a five
   month period in the nuclear medicine rooms (containing gamma-cameras),
   injection corridor, patient waiting areas, PET/CT study room, hot lab,
   waste storage room and corridors next to the hospitalization rooms for
   patients treated with I-131 and Cs-137. High dose values were found at
   the waste storage room, outside corridor of Cs-137 brachytherapy
   patients and PET/CT area. Ambient dose equivalent rate obtained for the
   Cs-137 brachytherapy corridor is equal to (18.51 +/- 0.02) x 10(-3)
   mSv/h. Sites with minimum doses are the gamma camera rooms, having
   ambient dose equivalent rates equal to (0.05 +/- 0.03) x 10(-3) mSv/h.
   Recommendations have been given to the Department authorities so that
   further actions are taken to reduce doses at high dose sites in order to
   comply with the ALARA principle (as low as reasonably achievable).},
