   Navarro-Sigueenza, Adolfo G. and Benitez-Diaz, Hesiquio and Peterson, A.
   Townsend},
Title = {Phylogeographic patterns of differentiation in the Acorn Woodpecker},
Journal = {WILSON JOURNAL OF ORNITHOLOGY},
Year = {2008},
Volume = {120},
Number = {3},
Pages = {478-493},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {Acorn Woodpecker (Melanerpes formicicorus) populations were sampled to
   evaluate geographic patterns of differentiation and connectivity across
   the species' range. We observed patterns of differentiation generally
   coincident with geographic patterns in plumage patterns with distinct
   subpopulations in Baja California Sur, northern Central America,
   Southern Central America. and mainland Mexico north into the
   southwestern United States. We confirmed the existence of geographic
   genetic structuring of populations of this species. although shared
   haplotypes between Baja California Sur and mainland Mexico Suggest that
   lineage sorting is not yet complete. The process of geographic
   differentiation and speciation is likely still underway in this group.},
